  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In a brief moment of chaos, Max realizes he needs to take control of a difficult situation. He tells his colleagues that they will figure it out and urges them to go. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ладно, мы разберёмся с этим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Всё будет хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что нам делать сейчас?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Поехали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Куда мы едем?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы никуда не едем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это значит, что мы начинаем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Теперь я всё понял.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Тогда давайте работать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, за работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "У нас мало времени.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Мы должны успеть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Конечно мы успеем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тогда начинаем прямо сейчас.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ладно, мы разберёмся с этим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, we will figure this out.' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'will sort this out' turns the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Всё будет хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everything will be fine.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'everything' with 'good'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что нам делать сейчас?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What should we do now?' Ilya combines 'what' with 'now' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Поехали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's go.' Max replies with a single verb form meaning 'here we go' — in Russian, the verb ending alone carries the subject and tense.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Куда мы едем?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where are we going?' The question opens with the interrogative word for 'where to' and builds toward 'are going'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы никуда не едем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We are not going anywhere.' Notice how 'we' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'are going'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это значит, что мы начинаем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It means that we are starting.' Notice how 'this' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'are starting'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Теперь я всё понял.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Now I understand everything.' Ilya combines 'now' with 'understood?' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Sasha responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Тогда давайте работать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then let's work.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'back then' with 'to work'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, за работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, back to work.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'yes' directly with 'job'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "У нас мало времени.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We have little time.' Ilya links 'at' directly with 'us' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Мы должны успеть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We must be in time.' Sasha combines 'we' with 'to make it' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Конечно мы успеем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Of course we will be in time.' Max links 'of course' directly with 'we' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тогда начинаем прямо сейчас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then we start right now.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'back then' with 'now'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
